// lexdb over TNS: TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE keeps the offset it was written with (design/lexdb-robust.md item 9, Val.Tsz),
// checked against Oracle Database Free 23.26 (gen_oracle.py --check tns_tstz; the known differences are in oracle_known_diffs.txt):
// a column of mixed offsets (TO_CHAR with TZH, TZM, TZR, TZD shows each value's own), compared, sorted, grouped, DISTINCTed, joined,
// indexed (a unique index) and found by instant; literals, FROM_TZ, SYS_EXTRACT_UTC, AT TIME ZONE and AT LOCAL, EXTRACT of the
// fields (UTC) and of TIMEZONE_HOUR and TIMEZONE_MINUTE, TZ_OFFSET, NEW_TIME, TO_TIMESTAMP_TZ, casts to and from TIMESTAMP, DATE and
// VARCHAR2, TIMESTAMP(0), subtraction (an interval), addition of an interval or a number of days (keeps the offset), SYSTIMESTAMP
// and CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, the session's time zone (ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE, SESSIONTIMEZONE) and NLS_TIMESTAMP_TZ_FORMAT, the
// errors of a bad zone (ORA-01874, 01875, 01882, 01857). A time zone by region name keeps the offset it had (TZR shows it), not the
// region: see oracle_known_diffs.txt. Each statement runs with set lexdb_batch = off (the row engine) and on: "same" and the
// answer, or DIFFERENT and both.
